{"name":"Honey Bee","hex":"#fcdfa4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honey bee","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many subspecies of bee, in seven species of genus Apis, often kept commercially for honey, beeswax, and pollination of crops."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honey_bee"}],"entries":[{"title":"Honey bee","blocks":[{"text":"A honey bee is a eusocial flying insect from the genus Apis of the largest bee family, Apidae. Honey bees are known for their construction of perennial nests within cavities containing hexagonal cells made of secreted wax, their large colony sizes, and their routine regurgitation of digested carbohydrates as surplus food storage in the form of honey, the lattermost of which distinguishes their hives as a prized foraging target of many mellivorous animals including honey badgers, bears and human hunter-gatherers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/The_Lone_Pollinator.jpg/330px-The_Lone_Pollinator.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_bee","wikidata":"Q102857"},{"title":"Honey Bee (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Honey Bee\" is a song written by Rhett Akins and Ben Hayslip and recorded by American country music artist Blake Shelton. It was released in April 2011 as the first single from Shelton's 2011 album Red River Blue. The song reached number one on the US Billboard Hot Country Songs chart in June 2011. On November 30, the song received a Grammy Award nomination for Best Country Solo Performance, but it lost to Taylor Swift's \"Mean\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e4/Blake_Shelton_-_Honey_Bee_Lyrics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_Bee_(song)","wikidata":"Q5893427"},{"title":"Honey","blocks":[{"text":"Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Runny_hunny.jpg/330px-Runny_hunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey","wikidata":"Q10987"},{"title":"Bee","blocks":[{"text":"Bees are winged insects that form a monophyletic clade Anthophila within the superfamily Apoidea of the order Hymenoptera, with over 20,000 known species in seven recognized families. Some species – including honey bees, bumblebees, and stingless bees – are social insects living in colonies, whereas over 90% of bee species – including mason bees, carpenter bees, leafcutter bees, and sweat bees – are solitary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg/330px-Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bee","wikidata":"Q7391"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Western_honey_bee_on_a_honeycomb.jpg/500px-Western_honey_bee_on_a_honeycomb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Western_honey_bee_on_a_honeycomb.jpg/1280px-Western_honey_bee_on_a_honeycomb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Western_honey_bee_on_a_honeycomb.jpg/500px-Western_honey_bee_on_a_honeycomb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Western honey bee on a honeycomb","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWestern_honey_bee_on_a_honeycomb.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Apis_mellifera_Tanzania.jpg/500px-Apis_mellifera_Tanzania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Apis_mellifera_Tanzania.jpg/500px-Apis_mellifera_Tanzania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The European honey bee may have originated from eastern Africa. This bee is pictured in Tanzania.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApis_mellifera_Tanzania.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Apis_mellifera_scutellata_1355020.jpg/500px-Apis_mellifera_scutellata_1355020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Apis_mellifera_scutellata_1355020.jpg/1280px-Apis_mellifera_scutellata_1355020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Apis_mellifera_scutellata_1355020.jpg/500px-Apis_mellifera_scutellata_1355020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Africanized honey bee (left) and a European honey bee on a honeycomb","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApis_mellifera_scutellata_1355020.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Anhui_Geological_Museum_20240811_145756_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Anhui_Geological_Museum_20240811_145756_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Anhui_Geological_Muse